      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Most conventional hypothesis tests involving ANOVA assume normality and homogeneity of error variances. Thus, prior to embarking on an analysis of variance, an attempt should always be made to ensure that the observed data are on an appropriate scale of measurement (Chapter 11). It should be realized, however, that normality of the observed data does not guarantee normality of the distributions of the underlying factors $ s_{i} $ and $ e_{ii} $.",
      "source_page": 574,
      "printed_page": 554,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter18:p008:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        127.0,
        489.0,
